omparison between two standard narcotic analgesics (morphine and meperidine) Pain (Dolor) 1987;2:55–61. [ Google Scholar ] 22. Temple RJ, Jones JK, Crout JR. Adverse effects of newly marketed drugs. N Engl J Med. 1979;300:1046–7. doi: 10.1056/NEJM197905033001810. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Rucker TD. Drug utilization review: Guidelines for program development. In: Alloza JL, editor. Clinical and Social Pharmacology: Postmarketing Period. Aulendorf, Germany: Editio Cantor; 1985. pp. 57–65. [ Google Scholar ] 24. Sriwatanakul K, Weis OF, Alloza JL, Kelvie W, Weintraub M, Lasagna L. Analysis of narcotic analgesic usage in the treatment of postoperative pain. JAMA. 1983;250:926–9. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Weintraub M. Intelligent noncompliance with special emphasis on the elderly. Contemp Pharm Pract. 1981;4:8–11. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Rucker TD. The formulary process at it relates to drug utilization review. Pharmacy and Therapeutics; 1992;17:646–57. [ Google Scholar ] 27. Mashford ML. Drug information and therapeutic guidelines: A second opinion before prescribing. In: Alloza JL, editor. Communication in Health Care: Drug Information. III. International Medical Pharmaceutical Meeting. Madrid, Spain: Madrid Doctors’ Association; 1995. pp. 12–6. [ Google Scholar ] 28. Tolpin HG. Cost-benefit assessment of pharmaceutical products. Drug Inf J. 1988;22:281–92. [ Google Scholar ] 29. Gibbong JH. Washington, DC: U.S. Congress, Office of Technology Assessment; 1992. Evaluation of the Oregon Medicaid Proposal. [ Google Scholar ] 30. Yan Q, Hunt CA. Preventing adverse drug events (ADEs): The role of computer information sys-tems. Drug Inf J. 2000;34:1247–60. [ Google Scholar ] 31. Marshall A. Laying the foundations for personalized medicines. Nat Biotechnol. 1997;15:954–7. doi: 10.1038/nbt1097-954. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 32. Maciá Bobes C, Begoña Paredes Ojanguren M, Pilar Poncela Cuesta M, Asunción Alvarez Rueda M, Castaño Fernández G, Venta Obaya R. Serum cortisol concentrations in acute non-critical ill patients, measured in three periods of the day. Med Clin (Barc) 2001;117:254–6. doi: 10.1016/s0025-7753(01)72078-7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 33. Rylance GW. Prescribing for infants and children. Br Med J (Clin Res Ed) 1988;296:984–6. doi: 10.1136/bmj.296.6627.984. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 34. Leape LL, Woods DD, Hatlie MJ, Kizer KW, Schroeder SA, Lundberg GD. Promoting patient safety by preventing medical error. JAMA. 1998;280:1444–7. doi: 10.1001/jama.280.16.1444. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 35. Conlan MF. Compounding vs manufacturing. Where is the line? Drug Top. 1992 Oct;:46–52. [ Google Scholar ] 36. Consumer Reports; 1999. Herbal prescriptions. The promises and pitfalls; pp. 44–8. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 37. Hufford MR. Placebo effects, memory, and the value of real-time data in drug development. App Clin Trials. 2002 Aug;:34–43. [ Google Scholar ] 38. Alloza JL, Alamo C, Espada M. Analysis of ambiguous terms widely used in the communication process among physicians, pharmacists and patients: A social pharmacology study. Methods Find Exp Clin Pharmacol. 2000;22:464. [ Google Scholar ] 39. Alloza JL, Lasagna L. A comparison of drug product information in four national compendia. Clin Pharmacol Ther. 1983;33:269–77. doi: 10.1038/clpt.1983.32. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 40. Moynihan R, Bero L, Ross-Degnan D, Henry D, Lee K, Watkins J, et al. Coverage by the news media of the benefits and risks of medications. N Engl J Med. 2000;342:1645–50. doi: 10.1056/NEJM200006013422206. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Szalavitz M. Web sites and misinformation about illicit drugs. N Engl J Med. 2001;345:1710. doi: 10.1056/NEJM200112063452316. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 42. Alloza JL. Social pharmacology of prescribing antidepressants in unregistered indications from the perspective of global medicine. Rev