, Cravero JP. Should I irradiate with computed tomography or sedate for magnetic resonance imaging? Pediatr Radiol. 2022;52:340–344. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 40. Hassan N, Butler C, DeCou J, et al. Pediatric cervical spine injuries on CT: difference in accuracy of interpretations by pediatric versus non-pediatric radiologists. Emerg Radiol. 2020;27:185–190. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Chen J, Majercik S, Bledsoe J, et al. The prevalence and impact of defensive medicine in the radiographic workup of the trauma patient: a pilot study. Am J Surg. 2015;210:462–467. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 42. Tong GE, Staudenmayer K, Lin F, et al. Use of emergency department imaging in patients with minor trauma. J Surg Res. 2016;203:238–245. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 43. Cattell HS, Filtzer DL. Pseudosubluxation and other normal variations in the cervical spine in children: a study of one hundred and sixty chiLDREN. J Bone Joint Surg Am. 1965;47:1295–1309. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 44. Junewick JJ. Pediatric craniocervical junction injuries. AJR. Am J Roentgenol. 2011;196:1003–1010. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',19,'2025-10-15 10:05:09','2025-10-15 10:05:09');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4629,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8055157','Late non-physiological impacts of Covid-19 on radiography education.','McConnell J, McFadden S, Floyd M, Elshami W, Abuzaid MM, Leishman L, Eckloff K.','2021','congratulate anxieties beneficially hcpc hypothesise preceptorships recoups weapon leaver retirements educationally watchful yours rainford zanardo buissinck decoster hennessy saravanan mahmoud elshami taha psyt elshami akudjedu abuzaid tekin cavli radi2020 mulla gauteng radi2020 zervides sassi kefala karli sassis questionnair radi2020 stogiannos fotopoulos woznitza malamateniou zanardo cattaneo ciaralli cornacchione bakar heng sustainably reorganise e303 e311 mohakud ranjan warriors murphyf radi2020 cushen strudwick doolan hcpc courtier mundy chivers registrants hcpc radi2020 infographic wfppmo scot binaries govscot govscot 3adocument scor kotecha traumatised','Dear Editor in Chief, We congratulate contributors to ‘Radiography’ on providing international insights into staff and student radiographer attitudes and experiences for safe clinical working and learning during the first wave of the pandemic. 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 , 7 , 8 , 9 Through identifying concerns around student experiences, positive responses have been outlined by universities through the adoption of synchronous and asynchronous online education and additional simulation activities to replace lost clinical time. 10 Literature has also recognised both staff and student anxieties experienced whilst adjusting to new ways of working during the first phase of the pandemic. 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 Research has shown there is noteworthy resilience and concern within student cohorts 11 , 12 , 13 . The latest global spike of the pandemic further influences departmental operations, resulting in an extension to adjusted work approaches. This, in our collective understanding, is impacting on students’ clinical exposure and their relationship with staff in supporting their clinical learning. Compared with the first spike in the pandemic, students may have experienced a significant impact on their clinical experience, making the application of the beneficially reported UK HCPC emergency register more difficult to use safely this year. 12 Consequently, we hypothesise that longer preceptorships may be necessary, as the almost qualified student recoups the lost or adjusted clinical experience. There are other long term questions to consider that have not yet been published/investigated, to address demands on staff and students during pandemic recovery. Vaccination is the main weapon of dealing with the pandemic in a ‘return to normal’ outlook; however this is by no means complete with new variants or infection spikes potentially impacting upon wider healthcare demands. Waiting times are reported as increasing significantly, though focussed demand on acute events or cancer treatment are now being addressed as services initiate their recovery strategies. 14 Consequently overall clinical learning may continue to be affected. To deal with the continued effects of the pandemic on radiography education, several questions need to be answered: • Is ‘burnout’ affecting teaching staff who are delivering virtual learning? • Is clinical experience of students affected, and does it continue to influence student preparation? • Has secondary school and college assessment impacted on school leaver performance at university, and could this influence course or profession retention? • How far has the agenda for service development been hampered through loss of postgraduate education, notwithstanding the positive impact of on-line delivery? 15 , 16 , 17 • Is clinical staff ‘burnout’ impacting students'' clinical experience? 18 • Will there be a future issue in recruitment to the profession? • Could an increase in mature student enrolment impact the workforce several years in the future through increased clinical staff retirements when this was not expected? • What might the positive and negative factors be in recruiting more mature students for service delivery and educationally? • What has the profession learned for local, regional and even national service delivery strategies and resilience generation? With our current and proposed pandemic learning, the radiography professional bodies must retain a watchful eye on developments for continued strategic planning to maintain profession and service resilience. Perhaps a special issue of ‘Radiography’ could address these ideas? Yours sincerely, Conflict of interest statement None. References 1. Rainford L.A., Zanardo M., Buissinck C., Decoster R., Hennessy W., Knapp K. The impact of COVID-19 upon student radiographers and clinical training. Radiography. 2020 doi: 10.1016/j.radi.2020.10.015. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Saravanan C., Mahmoud I., Elshami W., Taha M.H. Knowledge, anxiety, fear and psychological distress about COVID-19 among university students in the United Arab Emirates. Front Psychiatr. 2020;11 doi: 10.3389/f.psyt.2020.582189. 582189. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Elshami W., Akudjedu T.N., Abuzaid M., David LR., Tekin HO., Cavli B., et al The radiology workforce''s response to the COVID-19 pandemic in the Middle East, North Africa and India. Radiography https://doi.org/10.1016/j.radi2020.09.016 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 4. Lewis S. and Mulla F. Diagnostic radiographers'' experience of COVID-19, Gauteng South Africa. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.radi2020.09.009 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 5. Zervides C., Sassi M., Kefala-Karli P., Sassis L. Impact of COVID-19 pandemic on radiographers in the Republic of Cyprus. A Questionnair Surv. 2020 doi: 10.1016/j.radi2020.10.004. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Stogiannos N., Fotopoulos D., Woznitza N., Malamateniou C. COVID-19 in the radiology department: what radiographers need to know. Radiography. 2020;26:254–263. doi: 10.1016/j.radi.2020.05.012. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Zanardo M., Martini C., Monti C.B., Cattaneo F., Ciaralli C., Cornacchione P. Management of patients with suspected or confirmed COVID-19. Radiography. 2020;26:264–268. doi: 10.1016/j.radi.2020.04.010. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Sim W.Y., Chen R.C., Aw L.P., Abu Bakar R., Tan C.C., Heng A.L. How to safely and sustainably reorganise a large general radiography service facing the COVID-19 pandemic. Radiography. 2020;26:e303–e311. doi: 10.1016/j.radi.2020.05.001. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Mohakud S., Ranjan A., Naik S., Deep N. Letter to the Editor. COVID-19 preparedness for portable x-rays in an Indian hospital - safety of the radiographers, the frontline warriors. Radiography. 2020;26:270–271. doi: 10.1016/j.radi.2020.04.008. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Higgins R., MurphyF, Hogg P. The impact of teaching experimental research on-line:Research-informed teaching and COVID-19. Radiography. 2020 doi: 10.1016/j.radi2020.11.014. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Cushen-Brewster N., Strudwick R.M., Doolan C., Driscoll-Evans P. An evaluation of the experiences of radiography students working on the temporary HCPC register during the COVID-19 pandemic. Radiography. 2021 doi: 10.1016/j.radi.2021.03.003. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Courtier N., Brown P., Mundy L., Pope E., Chivers E., Williamson K. Expectations of therapeutic radiography students in Wales about transitioning to practice during the Covid-19 pandemic as registrants on the HCPC temporary register. Radiography. 2020 doi: 10.1016/j.radi2020.09.001. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Health Education England. ‘The impact of Covid-19 on students’ survey key findings infographic.pdf viewed on: Covid-19 updates - all professions (October 2020 onwards) | Health Education England (hee.nhs.uk) accessed March 2021. 14. Lynch P. BBC; 2020. Covid: cancer scan backlog raises late detection fears. [ Google Scholar ] 15. WFPPMO Scottish Government Integrated health and social care, workforce planning for Scotland: illustrative scenarios 16 December 2019. https://www.gov.scot/binaries/content/documents/govscot/publications/publication/2019/12/national-health-social-care-integrated-workforce-plan/documents/guidance/guidance/govscot%3Adocument/guidance.pdf Viewed November 2020. 16. Richards M. NHS; England: October, 2020. Chair independent review of diagnostic services for NHS England. Diagnostics: recovery and renewal. [ Google Scholar ] 17. The Society of Radiographers A career in radiography. https://www.sor.org/about-radiography/career-radiography Accessed December 2020. Society and College of Radiographers Diagnostic Radiography Workforce Census 2019. SCoR London, May. 18. Kotecha S. Covid: many NHS staff ''traumatised'' by first wave of virus, study shows. BBC News. 13 January 2021. https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/health-55630157 Accessed February 2021.',26,'2025-10-15 10:05:12','2025-10-15 10:05:12');
