INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4640,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10626871','Introduction to the COG Diagnostic Imaging Committee/SPR Oncology Committee White Papers: Rationale and methods.','Tang ER, Alazraki AL, Thacker PG, McCarville MB, Towbin AJ.','2023','endeavored authoring fran endeavored u10ca180886 mbm lrt zoltan patay untimely memoriam honoring patay aspnr aspnr inclusiveness aspnr aspnr resubmitted aspnr resubmission aspnr fleet endeavored aspnr aspnr weiser kaste barrington somer brisse mccarville granata neuroblastic fangusaro hernaiz driever rapno e305 chavhan schooler towbin podberesky gadoxetate disodium schooler squires alazraki towbin woodley pretext phitt poussaint vezina rapno mccarville memoriam zoltan patay e30092 pmk backus towbin e30362','Abstract Pediatric cancer is a rare disease. Because of this, many sites do not have experience providing imaging for specific tumor types. The Children’s Oncology Group Diagnostic Imaging Committee and the Society for Pediatric Radiology Oncology Committee are comprised of radiologists with expertise in pediatric cancer imaging. Recently, this group endeavored to create a series of twenty-three white papers designed to provide evidence-based imaging recommendations and minimum achievable imaging protocols. The purpose of this manuscript is to describe the methods employed in authoring the white paper series. Keywords: Cancer imaging, oncologic imaging, pediatric cancer Introduction In 2021, approximately 15,500 children were diagnosed with cancer in the United States ( 1 ). While this number is high, diagnosing a child with cancer is an uncommon event for most pediatric radiologists. Even the largest pediatric radiology practices may only diagnose a new malignancy in 40 to 60 patients per year. Thus, most pediatric radiologists do not have extensive experience in developing imaging protocols, recommending appropriate imaging studies, or interpreting scans for pediatric cancer patients. The Children’s Oncology Group (COG) Diagnostic Imaging Committee is comprised of leaders within the pediatric oncologic imaging community. This committee has been involved in pediatric cancer trials since the cooperative group’s inception in 2000. In more than 2 decades, COG research protocols have collected imaging for over 30,000 patients, including over 50,000 CTs, 30,000 MRIs, 10,000 PET scans, and 6,000 iodine-123 meta-iodobenzylguanidine scans [email communication from Fran Laurie, administrator for the Imaging and Radiation Oncology Core, Rhode Island]. While COG radiologists have considerable experience, this experience has not always translated to the general radiology practice. A 2013 Society for Pediatric Radiology (SPR) and COG Joint Task Force Report noted the challenges to research and clinical care posed by the lack of evidence-based imaging guidelines and by the variation in COG protocol-based imaging recommendations ( 2 ). Since then, protocols for various pediatric cancers have continued to evolve and new imaging techniques, new staging systems, and new response assessment criteria have all been developed, introducing new complexities ( 3 – 11 ). Recently, the SPR Oncology Committee surveyed the Society’s membership and found that more than 80% of the 115 survey respondents stated that variation in imaging affected the interpretation of pediatric cancer imaging at least sometimes (unpublished data). To address the lack of evidence-based pediatric oncologic imaging guidelines, the COG Diagnostic Imaging Committee, in partnership with the SPR Oncology Imaging Committee, endeavored to 1) identify if the lack of standardized imaging impacted pediatric cancer imaging, and 2) author a series of white papers outlining current imaging recommendations and minimum achievable imaging protocols for children with known or suspected malignancies. The purpose of this manuscript is to describe the methodology used by authors of the COG Diagnostic Imaging Committee/SPR Oncology Committee white papers. All white paper manuscripts (including this one) were funded in part by the National Clinical Trials Network Operations Center Grant U10CA180886. The content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the National Institutes of Health. Additionally, the publication of the white papers was funded in part by the SPR. Method for white paper creation and review: The Chair of the COG Diagnostic Imaging Committee [AJT] convened an Editorial Task Force to identify white paper topics, develop the format for each white paper and its tables, and identify potential authors. The editorial task force was composed of six pediatric radiologists [AJT, ALA, MBM, LRT, PGT, and ZP], each with experience in pediatric cancer imaging. The task force was designed to represent a diverse mix of institutional experience and clinical expertise. The editorial task force met regularly throughout the project. The Editorial Task Force would like to acknowledge the substantial contributions of Dr. Zoltán Patay who was a member of the Task Force until his untimely death in July of 2022. An In Memoriam honoring Dr. Patay is published in this supplement ( 12 ). First, the group agreed on guiding principles for white paper development. These principles included giving thorough, well-described imaging recommendations based on a standardized approach, promoting evidence-based assessments, and being inclusive of all pediatric cancer-imaging experts who wished to participate. To provide a complete overview of pediatric cancer-imaging, the Editorial Task Force identified 24 potential white paper topics. The identified topics were designed to cover all body parts and the majority of pediatric malignancies within each body system. After topics were identified, the Editorial Task Force created criteria for the ideal manuscript outline. This outline was made up of a series of 3 sections (background information related to the tumor(s), imaging recommendations for the tumor(s), and future directions for imaging the tumor(s)) and 17 questions to consider. The sections and questions are included in Table 1 . In addition to answering a series of standard questions, authorship teams were asked to complete standard tables outlining minimum achievable imaging protocol(s) for their tumor(s). As the imaging protocols and parameters were documented, authors were instructed to ensure that the protocols could be performed using machines as old as 10 years of age. This guidance was provided to ensure that the recommended imaging could be performed in the general radiology community. Table 1: Defined structure for COG Diagnostic Imaging Committee/SPR Oncology Committee and COG Diagnostic Imaging Committee/SPR Oncology Committee/ASPNR White Papers. TOPIC 1: Background Information Information to include Common tumors (if body-part-based) / common body parts involved (if disease-based) Incidence of disease Presenting signs and symptoms, especially as this guides imaging selection at the time of diagnosis QUESTION GROUP 1: Tumor Staging Which staging systems are used to assess the tumor? What role does imaging play in staging? Has the staging system been validated to correlate with survival outcomes (examples: overall survival, 5-year survival, or event-free survival)? TOPIC 2: Imaging Recommendations Information to include Advantages/disadvantages of each modality used to image the disease. QUESTION GROUP 2: Imaging at diagnosis Which imaging modality or modalities are recommended at diagnosis (include all studies required for staging - like chest CT for lung metastases)? If multiple imaging modalities can be used to assess the tumor, how would you order the modalities in terms of preference or ability to make a diagnosis? How does each modality contribute to staging/diagnostic work-up? What imaging parameters (field of view, contrast, radiopharmaceutical, slice thickness, etc) are recommended for optimal imaging with each modality? QUESTION GROUP 3: Imaging during follow-up Which imaging modality or modalities are recommended during follow-up? Do imaging parameters change from the imaging performed at the time of diagnosis? QUESTION GROUP 4: Imaging response assessment What criteria are used for tumor response assessment? Have the response assessment criteria been validated for this tumor or body part in children? QUESTION GROUP 5: Imaging surveillance Which imaging modality or modalities are recommended off therapy/during surveillance? Do the goals of imaging change with surveillance imaging? Are there late effects that change the goal of surveillance imaging? TOPIC 3: Future Directions QUESTION GROUP 6: Next generation imaging How will imaging of this tumor or body part change in the next 5–10 years? Are there new techniques that will enable more quantitative imaging in the future? Open in a new tab Because the white papers were designed to provide evidence-based recommendations, a standard evaluation schema was created. The Editorial Task Force created a two-step process for each recommendation. First, the authors were to critically appraise the literature. After that, the authors were to describe the strength of their recommendation. The quality of the literature was assessed using the Grading of Recommendations, Assessment, Development, and Evaluation (GRADE) system ( 13 , 14 ). In this system, GRADE A is considered high-quality evidence while GRADE D is very low-quality evidence ( 14 ). The GRADE system and its criteria are included in Table 2 . For each manuscript, the first author, second author, and last author were responsible for appraising the literature and applying the appropriate GRADE score. Table 2: Scoring System for Imaging Recommendations Made by the COG Diagnostic Imaging Committee/SPR Oncology Committee and COG Diagnostic Imaging Committee/SPR Oncology Committee/ASPNR White Papers. Modified from [9, 13]. GRADE Quality of evidence Description Types of evidence A High Further research is very unlikely to change our confidence in the estimate of effect Several high-quality studies with consistent results One or more large, high-quality multi-center trials One or more randomized controlled trials B Moderate Further research is likely to have an important impact on our confidence in the estimate of effect and may change the estimate One high-quality study Several studies with some limitations C Low Further research is very likely to have an important impact on our confidence in the estimate of effect and is likely to change the estimate One or more studies with severe limitations D Very low Any estimate of effect is very uncertain Expert opinion No direct research evidence One or more studies with very severe limitations Open in a new tab Next, a standard Strength of Recommendation (SOR) system was adopted ( 9 ). In this system, each author was required to independently describe their own agreement with the recommendation using a 1 through 4 Likert scale. A score of 1 was defined as a Very Strong recommendation while score of 4 was defined as a Weak recommendation. The overall SOR was calculated as the arithmetic mean of the individual scores. As previously described, an overall SOR score of 1–1.49 was considered a Very Strong recommendation, a score of 1.5–1.99 was considered a Strong recommendation, a score of 2–2.99 was considered a Moderate recommendation, and a score of 3 and above was considered a Weak recommendation ( 9 ). In order to address the guiding principle of inclusiveness, a call for interested authors was sent via email to all members of the COG Diagnostic Imaging Committee and the SPR Oncology Committee. Through this call for participation, interested members were asked to complete a survey stating their preferences for tumor or body part specific white paper involvement. Members were asked first if they wanted to participate, and if so, to identify and rank up to three topics of interest. Additionally, survey respondents were asked if they wished to be considered for a role as a primary author or senior author. If they wished to be involved as a lead author (either primary or senior) they were asked to identify and rank the topics that they wished to lead. The Editorial Task Force reviewed the survey responses and created the authorship teams. Initially, the first authors and senior authors were assigned. During this process, the Editorial Task Force set goals for each lead author position. The first author position was ideally assigned to junior committee members experienced with the specific tumor(s) or body part. The senior author position was ideally filled with members who had been involved in COG trials or those who had extensive experience with the specific tumor(s) or body part. After the first and senior author positions were selected, additional authors were added to each white paper team. Additional authors were added by prioritizing survey respondents’ choice while balancing authorship group size. This balance helped to ensure a diverse mix of pediatric radiologists with relevant expertise in the respective topic for each authorship team. After the authors were assigned, an Editorial Task Force member was assigned as a liaison with each authorship team. Once authorship teams were confirmed, an email was sent by the chair of the Editorial Task Force to the first and senior authors describing the project in more detail. This email described the goals of the white paper, the format to be used when writing, the methodology for appraising the literature and providing recommendations, and the authorship responsibilities. Two online community forums were held for the author leads. These forums provided a mechanism for the primary and senior authors to ask questions related to their responsibilities. Additionally, the forum allowed the Editorial Task Force Chair to further describe the white paper process and provide the rationale for the standard questions and tables. The lead authors were encouraged to continue to ask questions via email. These questions could be directed to either their Editorial Task Force liaison or the Task Force Chair. Each authorship team was given approximately one year to complete an initial draft of their manuscript. The white paper groups were given flexibility in how they conducted their meetings, how they identified the relevant literature, and how work was distributed within the group. Once the draft manuscript was complete, it was submitted to the assigned Editorial Task Force liaison. The liaison was responsible for editing the draft and providing feedback to the authors. The Editorial Task Force liaison and author leads worked in an iterative process until the work was sufficient for submission. Once complete, the manuscript was submitted to the Chair of the Editorial Task Force for approval by the COG and SPR, and, for four neuroradiology topics, the American Society of Pediatric Neuroradiology (ASPNR). The process for obtaining each group/society’s approval was different. The SPR’s Board of Directors provided broad approval for the white papers recognizing that the work was initiated and completed by its members with oversight from its Oncology Committee. The four neuro-imaging manuscripts were approved by the ASPNR. The Society created a White Paper Editorial Task Force to facilitate the process. This Task Force provided a detailed journal-like review and requested changes to each manuscript before final approval. Manuscripts were returned to the author leads. The authors performed or addressed all revision requests. Each manuscript was then resubmitted to the ASPNR White Paper Editorial Task Force for final approval. Finally, COG leadership individually approved each manuscript. The Editorial Task Force Chair submitted each manuscript via the Group’s online portal. The Group Chair, Diagnostic Imaging Committee Vice Chair, and Lead Group Statistician reviewed all manuscripts. During this process, each of these leaders could either approve the manuscript for submission, approve the manuscript pending minor edits, or ask for significant revisions and require resubmission. Manuscripts requiring edits were returned to the author leads. All edits were either performed or addressed. Purpose of White Papers The purpose of the COG Diagnostic Imaging Committee/SPR Oncology Committee and COG Diagnostic Imaging Committee/SPR Oncology Committee/ASPNR white papers is to provide expert guidance to radiologists, oncologists, and other medical professionals as they care for children with cancer. Specifically, the imaging protocols contained within each white paper are meant to provide minimum achievable imaging protocols. While these protocols can be used as recommended protocols in imaging trials, they should not be considered to represent required sequences in a trial ( 15 ). Additionally, the imaging protocols contained within the white papers should not be considered to represent the only parameters suitable for clinical care. Due to differences in age or capabilities of imaging equipment and software platforms between institutions some radiology departments will exceed the parameters and sequences described in this series of manuscripts. The local protocol may represent the optimal imaging currently achievable. Finally, while the white paper’s imaging protocols are designed to represent minimum achievable protocols, they should not be considered as the basis for standard-of-care. Institutions must make decisions related to imaging protocols based on multiple factors. These factors may include those related to the modality (e.g., age of the scanner, differences within the fleet of scanners, scanner capabilities), the schedule (e.g., available time for imaging), the institutional resources (e.g., availability of pediatric anesthesia, location of scanner in relation to other resources), and the patient (e.g., clinical symptoms, ability to undergo a specific imaging study, other studies needed). We encourage radiologists to evaluate their protocols, compare them to those contained within the white paper series, and update them if they are thought to be deficient. In addition to providing imaging protocols, the authors of each white paper provided a series of recommendations related to imaging, staging, and response assessment among other topics. While the authors endeavored to provide evidence-based recommendations, the quality of this evidence was often low (GRADE C) or very low (GRADE D). Thus, many recommendations represent expert opinion. The white paper authors recognize that different experts, with different backgrounds, training, or resources may make a different recommendation. We believe that this difference of opinion should form the basis for future research that, in turn, will provide scientific evidence on which to base imaging recommendations. Conclusion Until now, there has been a dearth of consensus guidance on the best imaging protocols for the pediatric oncology population. We believe that this lack of consensus has led to inconsistent imaging. Members of the COG Diagnostic Imaging Committee, SPR Oncology Committee, and ASPNR have worked together to create a vast array of site- and disease-specific imaging protocols for the diverse range of pediatric cancer. In addition to providing guidance related to the imaging technique, each white paper makes a series of evidence-based recommendations to guide those caring for pediatric cancer patients. The hope is that these white papers educate caregivers, guide trial development, and form the basis for future research. Abbreviations key COG Children’s Oncology Group CT Computed tomography MRI Magnetic resonance imaging PET Positron emission tomography SPR Society for Pediatric Radiology GRADE Grading of Recommendations, Assessment, Development, and Evaluation SOR Strength of recommendation ASPNR American Society of Pediatric Neuroradiology References