 summer at 45.00%, while household products and health/nutritional supplements showed less variation (24.06–28.71% and 22.23–24.46% respectively). Regional variation was modest, with medications comprising 47.82–50.04% across business units. Table 4 stratifies clinical features across agent categories among telehealth poisoning consultations, showing significant differences by exposure type ( p < 0.001). Home observation was recommended for most cases (71.14–87.76%), except bites/stings and substance abuse which more frequently required hospital referral (70.56% and 61.97% respectively) despite predominantly asymptomatic presentations (nearly over 93%). Supplements poisoning had the lowest symptom rate (1.39%) and highest home observation (87.76%). Table 4. Clinical characteristics by type of toxic exposure among telehealth toxicology consultations from public hotline calls ( N = 39,142) Variable Medicines N (%) Abuse Substances N (%) Health Supplements and Nutritional Supplements N (%) Bites and Stings N (%) Household Products N (%) Total N (%) P -value SPI Decision Home observation 13,748 (71.14) 108 (38.03) 7,897 (87.76) 68 (29.44) 7,356 (71.39) 29,172 (74.53) < 0.001 Go Hospital 5,577 (28.86) 176 (61.97) 1,101 (12.24) 163 (70.56) 2,948 (28.61) 9,970 (25.47) Symptoms Asymptomatic 18,828 (97.43) 264 (92.96) 8,873 (98.61) 219 (94.81) 9,858 (95.67) 38,042 (97.19) < 0.001 Symptomatic 497 (2.57) 20 (7.04) 125 (1.39) 12 (5.19) 446 (4.33) 1,100 (2.81) Total 19 , 325 (49.37) 284 (0.73) 8998 (22.99) 231 (0.59) 10 , 304 (26.32) 39 , 142 (100) Open in a new tab N: frequency; %: percentaṣge; SPI: Specialists in Poison Information Discussion This retrospective cross-sectional study analyzed national data from 39,142 public hotline toxicology consultations to characterize poisoning epidemiology and clinical profiles managed by telehealth in Saudi Arabia. Population-standardized utilization rates identified high-risk groups, with children under 5 years old having by far the highest consultation rates, peaking at 157.59 per 10,000 among 1-<3 years olds (Table 1 ). Medications predominated as the toxic exposure in nearly half of calls, followed by household chemicals and health/nutritional supplements (Table 2 ). Poisoning consultation patterns demonstrated large variations according to age, with most calls involving pediatric household exposures, while the predominance of medication incidents emerged mainly in older adults over 65 years. Modest variations were observed by sex, season, and region (Table 3 ). Clinically, most cases were managed with home observation, though bites/stings and substance abuse frequently warranted hospital referral despite predominantly asymptomatic presentations across agents (Table 4 ). Key findings indicate heightened pediatric poisoning risk, especially from medications, and agent category -specific differences in clinical profiles and management considerations relevant to telehealth triage. This nationally representative characterization of community-based poisoning consultations provides insights to guide prevention initiatives, optimize telehealth resources, and inform future prospective research. The high consultation rates among young children are consistent with previous studies in the region identifying early childhood as the most vulnerable period for unintentional poisonings [ 8 – 10 , 25 – 30 ]. This likely relates to developmental milestones including increased mobility, mouthing behaviors, and limited hazard awareness that enable encounters with medications and enticing household chemicals [ 31 , 32 ]. Multifaceted initiatives are needed to promote safe storage, childproofing, heightened supervision, and caregiver education during this stage [ 6 , 32 , 33 ]. Pediatricians should consistently counsel on risk and prevention strategies. Public messaging should target communities through campaigns and media. Implementing such upstream approaches can help reduce heavy utilization of TCSs resulting from developmental vulnerabilities in early childhood. Our findings reveal a striking predominance of medicine-related poisonings, aligning with prior research in Saudi Arabia and globally [ 8 – 10 , 34 , 35 ]. This pattern was especially pronounced among the elderly, underscoring the urgent need for age-targeted medication safety interventions in this high-risk group. Strategies should focus on proper storage, simplified dosing, and supervision to reduce accidental geriatric exposures [ 34 , 35 ]. Tailored approaches accounting for age-related behavioral risk factors are key to mitigating medication poisoning risks in older adults. While raw consultation numbers showed nearly equal distribution between males and females, population-adjusted rates revealed higher utilization among females, consistent with literature on female healthcare-seeking behavior [ 36 , 37 ]. We also noted sex variations in exposure types, with slightly more medicine and supplement poisonings in females but higher household chemical cases in males, mirroring established research [ 5 , 38 , 39 ]. Our results suggest poisoning risks may be equalizing between genders in Saudi Arabia, possibly reflecting societal changes. However, sex-tailored prevention strategies remain important in addressing the unique risk factors and exposure patterns in each group. Regional variations revealed higher poisoning consultations in Central and Western regions across most toxin categories. This aligns with their larger populations and urbanization increasing awareness and service access compared to rural areas [ 40 – 43 ]. The Northern region’s consistently lower rates, except for envenomations, may reflect limited exposures to toxins associated with urban living. A policy implication is the need to improve access to telehealth TCS and community awareness of poisoning risks and prevention in rural regions through targeted community health campaigns and engagement with local stakeholders. Establishing strategically located regional poison information centers with region-specific protocols would enhance response capabilities while addressing unique urban-rural toxin exposure patterns in accordance with local norms and needs. Ultimately, tailored prevention is needed across all regions based on population size, predominant toxins, and exposure risks. Poisoning prevention strategies considering local contexts provides the most promising path for evidence-based intervention. Our findings demonstrate the vital role of TCS expert triage in optimizing poisoning management while avoiding unnecessary healthcare visits [ 40 ]. By delivering individualized guidance aligned with exposure severity, the TCS facilitated care at the minimal appropriate level. This approach reduced costs, improved outcomes, and minimized anxiety compared to default emergency department referrals [ 17 , 44 , 45 ]. The rarity of severe cases signifies the TCS enabled expectant management for most exposures through remote expert evaluation. Overall, the TCS exemplifies how telehealth can improve efficiency and accessibility without compromising quality of care. The TCS provides a model of remote telehealth management of poisonings that could be adopted by healthcare systems worldwide. Centralized call centers with telephone access provide prompt evidence-based triage regardless of geography. This prevents unnecessary healthcare visits and maximizes distribution of resources aligned with clinical need. As Saudi Arabia has demonstrated, nationwide implementation of integrated telehealth systems enhances coordination and data-driven health policy development. Healthcare systems globally can gain invaluable benefits in efficiency, quality, and sustainability by investing specifically in centralized telehealth capabilities for poisoning and toxicology management, especially in resource-limited areas and during crisis [ 17 , 18 , 46 ]. The findings from this study have several important implications for health policy in Saudi Arabia. The high consultation rates among young children and predominance of medication-related exposures suggest a need for strengthened regulations on medication packaging and storage. Implementation of mandatory child-resistant packaging and unit-dose dispensing could reduce accidental pediatric exposures [ 47 ]. The regional variations in service utilization indicate a need for targeted resource allocation. Given the high proportion of cases safely managed through telehealth consultation, policies supporting expansion of remote toxicology services could improve healthcare efficiency while maintaining quality of care [ 17 , 18 , 46 ]. Limitations and Strengths This study has some limitations requiring consideration when interpreting the findings. The retrospective cross-sectional nature of the study limited the causal inference and assessment of long-term clinical outcomes and verification of final diagnoses. While providing valuable preliminary insights into observed associations between variables, the analytical approach relied on descriptive statistics and crude associations between variables. By focusing on public calls, the study may not represent the full spectrum of poisoning cases, particularly those presenting directly to healthcare facilities. Information accuracy relied on caller reports from heterogeneous regions with varying socio-behavioral patterns, which may contain bias or incomplete details affecting clinical outcome assessment. While population-standardized rates were calculated, important demographic variables such as socioeconomic status, education level, and detailed household characteristics were unavailable in the database. Additionally, the single-year timeframe may not capture longer-term trends or seasonal variations across multiple years. However, this study’s analysis of a large, nationally representative dataset of over 39,000 toxicology consultations provides comprehensive insights into poisoning patterns across Saudi Arabia, offering valuable epidemiological evidence to guide prevention strategies and resource allocation. Future Directions Future studies could develop a combined severity score by integrating individual severity variables to enable more comprehensive analysis of poisoning cases. This would account for varying toxicity thresholds across different substances while enabling analysis of severity patterns across socio-demographic characteristics. To strengthen the evidence base, future research could conduct longitudinal studies, employ more sophisticated analyses, integrate hospital-based data with poison center records, and develop predictive models to better understand exposure patterns. Studies could investigate associated risk factors using more sophisticated statistical approaches that account for confounding variables to identify predictors that increase toxin exposure across different age groups, regions, and sexes. These analyses would help policymakers develop targeted, evidence-based prevention strategies appropriate for each demographic. Conclusion This first comprehensive analysis of over 39,000 toxicology consultations reveals distinct epidemiological patterns, with young children aged 1-<3 years showing the highest consultation rates, indicating they are at greatest risk for unintentional poisonings. Medications predominated exposures across groups. The large proportion of asymptomatic cases managed via telehealth consultation demonstrates these services can potentially expand access and provide guidance without requiring in-person health facility visits. Qualitative research is needed to explore sociocultural factors influencing behaviors and exposures to inform tailored, localized prevention messaging and initiatives. Findings support implementing public health strategies like caregiver education on poison prevention and safe medication storage, utilizing child-resistant packaging, and increasing access to remote toxicology services. Provider education highlighting high-risk demographics could also inform preventative counseling. Overall, this nationwide characterization provides an evidence-base to target prevention efforts and policies focused on highest-risk groups. By guiding strategic resource allocation along with public health and clinical initiatives aligned to key findings, the health care system can curb poisoning incidence through integrated care models exemplified by the TCS. Acknowledgements The authors would like to thank the Saudi Medical Appointments and Referrals Center (SMARC) for their support in data collection. We also acknowledge the contributions of all healthcare professionals involved in the management of poisoning cases across Saudi Arabia. Author Contributions A.A.A., M.A.M., M.S.B., A.Y.A., A.K.A., M.S.A., A.A., Y.A.A., E.B.A., M.A.A., M.A., M.M.K., M.A., and N.A.A. contributed to the conception and design of the study. A.A.A., M.A.M., M.S.B., and A.Y.A. contributed to acquisition of data. A.A.A., M.A.M., M.S.B., A.Y.A., A.K.A., M.S.A., A.A., and Y.A.A. contributed to analysis and interpretation of data. A.A.A., M.A.M., M.S.B., and A.Y.A. drafted the manuscript. E.B.A., M.A.A., M.A., M.M.K., M.A., N.A.A., A.K.A., M.S.A., A.A., and Y.A.A. critically revised the manuscript for important intellectual content. All authors gave final approval of the version to be published. Funding This research received no external funding. Data Availability The raw dataset analyzed in this study are governmental health records not publicly available due to privacy restrictions. However, requests for aggregated or anonymized versions of the data may be considered by the corresponding author pending approval from the relevant national authorities. Declarations Institutional Review Board Statement The study was conducted according to the guidelines of the Declaration of Helsinki, and approved by the Institutional Review Board of the Saudi Ministry of Health (Ref: 23-77-E). Informed Consent Patient consent was wai