INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4652,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11721767','Assessment of renal insufficiency in patients with renal artery stenosis by multiparametric magnetic resonance imaging.','Zhao L, Xu FB, Liu JY, Li S, Zhang N, Sun ZH, Wang GQ, Xu L.','2025','ingenia variate 69auc coeffecient fibromuscular intervened allografts deoxyhaemoglobin arteriography arteriography isotopic isosk claustrophobia ingenia dstream axially bifurcations enc slab coronally coronally δte tis coronally pcasl coronally slab tsinghua segmenting slicer postprocessing postprocessing r2022a mathworks natick elastix nonrigid groupwise pcasl pcasl affine isosk corticomedullary isosk allografts isosk isosk makvandi schoenberg interstitium predicter isosk motions irnf_a_2444403_sm1991 irnf_a_2444403_sm1990 irnf_a_2444403_sm1989 irnf_a_2444403_sm1988 2022yfe0209800 folt renovasc ijnrd s40175 djl intrarenal laboi mamtora renovascular odudu nery harteveld suppl_2 ii15 ii21 gfy180 ringgaard magma s10334 artz wentland sadowski microsphere 0b013e3181f5e101 schoenberg knopp caroli friedli suppl_2 ii29 ii40 gfy163 suppl_2 ii41 ii50 gfy198 wormleighton pruijm milani pivin pruijm mendichovszky liss suppl_2 ii22 ii28 gfy243 nephrourology nephrourology halladay m16 bane ljimani caroli laustsen magma s10334 bane mendichovszky milani magma s10334 dekkers magma s10334 nery harteveld magma s10334 fedorov beichel kalpathy slicer staring elastix huizinga poot guyader groupwise caroli pruijm burnier parenchima suppl_2 ii1 ii3 gfy181 duggal renovascular tafur revascularize shurrab renovascular chrysochou atheromatous e0177178 chrysochou shurrab gfp623 sarafidis theodorakopoulou renovascular erbp gfad095 makvandi hockings sfac054 zha ultrahigh noninvasively juillard mahmoud gfz129 friedli berchtold srep30088 irnf_a_2444403_sm1991 irnf_a_2444403_sm1990 irnf_a_2444403_sm1989 irnf_a_2444403_sm1988','Abstract Objectives To evaluate the function of kidneys with renal artery stenosis using multiparametric magnetic resonance imaging, assess the diagnostic efficacy of multiparametric magnetic resonance imaging for single kidney dysfunction. Materials and methods Renal multiparametric magnetic resonance imaging was performed on 62 patients with RAS using the Philips Ingenia CX 3.0 T MRI machine. The scanning sequences included arterial spin labeling, phase contrast MRI, diffusion weighted imaging, T1 mapping, and blood oxygen level-dependent MRI. All patients underwent radionuclide renal dynamic imaging, and the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) was calculated to determine renal function. Individual kidneys from renal artery stenosis patients were classified into normal (GFR ≥ 30) and reduced (GFR < 30) groups and the ability of the uni- and multi-variate logistic regression model to predict the group was determined. Results MR parameters demonstrated considerable diagnostic efficacy for single kidney dysfunction, with AUC range of 0.597– 0.864. The strongest predictor was mean renal artery blood flow. The sensitivity and specificity were 0.93 and 0.69AUC was 0.864. The strongest predictors of the renal microstructure were cortical apparent diffusion coeffecient and T1 value, with ROC AUCs of 0.756 and 0.741, sensitivities of 0.875 and 0.689, and specificities of 0.537 and 0.731. Multiparametric MRI combined with the values of cortical renal blood flow and cortical T1 exhibited the highest diagnostic efficacy, with an AUC of 0.92, and sensitivity of 0.919, and specificity of 0.743. Conclusion Multiparametric magnetic resonance imaging can effectively detect the single renal dysfunction of kidneys with renal artery stenosis, which holds promise for the diagnosis and prognosis of patients with renal artery stenosis. Keywords: Multiparametric magnetic resonance imaging, renal artery stenosis, kidney function, glomerular filtration rate KEY POINTS Multiple MR parameters demonstrated good predictive ability for single kidney function impairment. The combined application of multiparametric MRI indicators enhance the diagnostic efficacy of renal function impairment in renal artery stenosis. The renal cortical RBF combining the cortical T1 value exhibited the highest diagnostic efficacy for renal function impairment. 1. Introduction Renal artery stenosis (RAS) causes decreased renal blood perfusion and activates the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system, which can lead to secondary hypertension, ischemic nephropathy, renal failure, and even death [ 1 ]. The main causes of RAS include atherosclerosis, fibromuscular dysplasia, and Takayasu arteritis. Atherosclerosis accounts for 90% of all cases of RAS [ 2 ]. Owing to population aging, RAS has received increasing attention as the main cause of secondary hypertension. RAS usually presents as an asymptomatic chronic disease at the initial stage. However, if it is not effectively intervened in an early stage, renal function is usually not restored even if the narrow arteries are opened at a later stage to restore renal blood flow [ 3 ]. Hence, timely evaluation and treatment of RAS are crucial. In recent years, research has shown that reductions in renal perfusion and function in patients with RAS are not closely linked to the degree of angiographic stenosis [ 4 ]. This finding indicates that the morphological change is not the only mechanism affecting the kidneys in RAS. On the contrary, a series of complex physiological changes in the kidneys and even the entire body may be involved when RAS occurs. Hence, the evaluation of patients with RAS should simultaneously focus on the functional status of renal parenchyma, which is of great significance for the treatment and prognosis of patients. Patients with renal artery stenosis (RAS) often experience varying degrees of renal function impairment, so caution should be exercised when using contrast agents. Non-Contrast magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a noninvasive, non-contrast agent, and non-ionizing radiation examination method that has expanded the options for RAS patients, but it has typically been used in isolation in the past. Multiparametric MRI can offer a comprehensive assessment of kidney structure and function. While this method has been successfully utilized in chronic kidney disease (CKD) and renal allografts, its application for assessing RAS remains unexplored. In this study, we utilized multiparametric MRI to assess kidneys with RAS. Multiple quantitative parameters were obtained through a single-scan procedure, including arterial spin labeling (ASL), phase contrast MRI (PC-MRI), diffusion weighted imaging (DWI), T1 mapping, and blood oxygen level-dependent MRI (BOLD MRI). ASL is an MRI technique that uses magnetic labeling of water in arterial blood as an endogenous tracer to generate maps of absolute regional perfusion without requiring exogenous contrast [ 5 ]. PC-MRI is a noninvasive method used to compute blood flow velocity and volume [ 6 ]. Both can offer insights into renal perfusion and hemodynamics [ 7 , 8 ]. DWI is a noninvasive method sensitive to local water motion in the tissue. As a method to detect the microstructure, DWI has the potential to become an effective imaging biomarker to exhibit the degree of renal fibrosis [ 9 ]. T1 measurements could be used to assess renal interstitial fibrosis. Previous studies show that T1 mapping has the potential to estimate renal function [ 10 ]. Both DWI and T1 mapping can be employed to observe the renal microstructure [ 11 , 12 ]. BOLD MRI can assist in evaluating renal tissue oxygenation via the T2* value [ 13 ]. Increases in its outcome measure R2* (transverse relaxation rate expressed as per second, R2*=1/T2*) correspond to higher deoxyhaemoglobin concentrations and suggest lower oxygenation, whereas decreases in R2* indicate higher oxygenation [ 14 ]. The glomerular filtration rate (GFR) acquired using radionuclide renal dynamic imaging is considered the gold standard for assessing patients’ renal function in clinical practice [ 15 , 16 ]. However, it has certain limitations due to its invasiveness and complex operational process. In this study, multiple MR parameters are used for comprehensive evaluation of single kidney dysfunction. This study aims to show multiparametric renal MRI parameters’ potential to detect reduced renal function in RAS patients, thereby enhancing early intervention and treatment for patients. 2. Materials and methods 2.1. Participants From October 2022 to May 2024, 62 patients with RAS were consecutively enrolled. General information was recorded for each patient. The inclusion criteria were as follows: (1) the patient had completed at least one renal artery examination according to which the degree of RAS was >50% [ 17 ] and was ready for renal arteriography; (2) The patient had undergone radionuclide renal dynamic imaging before renal arteriography, and the isotopic single kidney global filtration rate (isoSK-GFR) had been obtained. The exclusion criteria were as follows: (1) patients with acute kidney injury in in the past 6 months; (2) patients with CKD; (3) patients with diabetes; (4) patients who had received renal allograft; (5) patients with previous renal artery stenting; (6) patients with renal atrophy; (7) patients with artery occlusion; (8) patients with pregnancy, claustrophobia, or other contraindications to MR examination. Unilateral renal atrophy was identified in 6 of the 62 patients, and 118 kidneys were finally included. This prospective study was approved by the ethics committee, and all participants provided written informed consent. 2.2. Multiparametric renal MRI Multiparametric renal MRI was performed on a 3.0 T MR scanner (Ingenia CX, Philips Healthcare, Best, The Netherlands) with a 16-channel dStream Torso coil and a 12-channel embedded posterior coil. 2.2.1. T2-weighted (T2w) imaging A breath-hold T2 spectral attenuated inversion recovery sequence was acquired axially to determine the kidney volume. The imaging parameters were as follows: field of view (FOV) = 380 × 380 × 81 mm 3 , in-plane resolution = 1.8 × 1.8 mm 2 , slice thickness = 4 mm, 20 slices, repetition time (TR) = 362 ms, echo time (TE) = 80 ms, flip angle = 90°, and scan time = 7s. 2.2.2. Pc-MRI The imaging slice of the PC-MRI sequence was placed perpendicular to the trunk of the renal artery and prior to bifurcations according to the MR angiogram. Breath-hold PC MRI was implemented on both left and right renal arteries, with the following parameters [ 18 ]: FOV = 150 × 300 mm 2 , in-plane resolution = 1.17 × 1.17 mm 2 , slice thickness = 5 mm, flip angle = 25°, TR = 7.5 ms, TE = 4.3 ms, velocity encoding (V ENC ) = 150 cm/s, and scan time for each renal artery = 18 s. Acquisition heart phases 10.5, reconstructed heart phases 15. 2.2.3. DWI Respiratory-triggered fat-suppressed spin-echo EPI DWI data [ 19 ] were acquired at b-value = 1000. The imaging slab of the DWI was placed coronally to cover both kidneys based on the T2w images. Other parameters were as follows: sensitivity-encoding acceleration factor = 2.5, FOV = 380 × 380 × 84 mm 3 , in-plane resolution = 3 × 3.55 mm 2 , slice thickness = 8 mm, 20 slices, TR = 1142 ms, TE = 79 ms, flip angle = 90°, and scan time = 2 min 6s. 2.2.4. BOLD imaging A BOLD MRI was performed coronally with the following parameters [ 20 ]: FOV = 380 × 380 × 84 mm 3 , voxel = 2.5 × 3 mm 2 , slice thickness = 8 mm, 20 slices, TR = 416 ms, TE = 17 ms, ΔTE = 33.7 ms, 12 echoes, flip angle = 30°, breath-hold acquisition, scan time = 21 s. The planning of the BOLD sequence was the same as that of the DWI sequence. 2.2.5. T1 mapping A series of classical inversion recovery (IR) sequences complies with the international consensus-based technical recommendations of renal T1 mapping MRI with different inversion times (TIs) were acquired coronally [ 21 ]. TI = 350/550/750/950/1150/1350/1650 ms, FOV = 380 × 380 × 56 mm 3 , in-plane resolution = 2 × 2 mm 2 , slice thickness = 8 mm, 7 slices, TR = 4.4 ms, TE = 2.1 ms, flip angle = 6°, free-breathing acquisition, total scan time = 1 min 30 s. The center of the FOV was maintained the same between the inversion recovery sequences for T1 mapping and the DWI sequence. 2.2.6. ASL imaging Pseudo-continuous ASL (pCASL) images were acquired coronally [ 22 ]. The labeling plane was positioned 10 cm from the center of the kidneys in the superior direction. The center of the FOV was kept the same between the imaging slab of the single-delay ASL sequence and the DWI sequence. The main sequence parameters were as follows: label duration = 2000 ms, post-label delay = 2000 ms, FOV = 380 × 380 × 62 mm 3 , in-plane resolution = 3 × 3 mm 2 , slice thickness = 8 mm, 7 slices, flip angle = 70°, TR = 7700 ms, TE = 21 ms, free-breathing acquisition, scan time = 4 min 54 s. The acquisition protocol adopted in the study complies with the international recommendations. The previous study of sequence verification by Tsinghua University has been published [ 23 , 24 ]. 2.3. Image processing 2.3.1. Total kidney volume The parenchymal volume (PV) of the total kidney was measured by manually segmenting the kidney on T2w images using an open source software 3D Slicer (5.7.0) [ 25 ]. 2.3.2. Renal artery blood flow and global perfusion Renal artery blood flow was calculated with the PC-MRI sequence using the Q-flow software (Philips Medical Systems, Best, The Netherlands). A region of interest (ROI) was manually placed over each renal artery, and the parameters of stroke volume (mL), mean renal artery blood flow (mL/s), and mean velocity (cm/s) were recorded over the cardiac cycle. 2.3.3. DWI mapping and T2* mapping Apparent diffusion coefficient (ADC) maps were drawn on the Philips postprocessing platform using the following formula: (−1/b) log (S1/S0), where b is the diffusion factor, S0 is the signal intensity with b = 0, and S1 is the signal intensity after the application of the diffusion gradient. The T2* map was obtained from the BOLD images via T2* delay fitting on the Philips postprocessing platform. For the DWI and BOLD images, six circular regions were evenly outlined on the renal cortex and medulla of each kidney to calculate the mean ADC value and mean T2* value, respectively (Supplementary data, Figure S1 ). 2.3.4. The T1 and ASL perfusion mapping The T1 and ASL perfusion maps were analyzed using an in-house program on Matlab R2022a (The Mathworks, Natick, MA) with Elastix for image registration [ 26 ]. To minimize the effects of respiratory motion, a nonrigid principal component analysis-based (PCA-based) groupwise registration [ 27 ] was initially applied to the source images of all T1w and pCASL sequences. After registration, the T1 map was obtained from pixel-wise exponential recovery curve fitting of the registered T1w images. For pCASL images, an affine mutual information-based pairwise registration was performed for each slice to register the ASL images to the M0 images. Subsequently, renal blood flow (RBF) maps were quantified using the single-compartment model [ 28 ]. After parametric mapping, the renal cortex and medulla ROIs were manually extracted from the images and mean values of T1 and ­perfusion parameters were calculated. 2.4. Statistical analysis SPSS 20.0 statistical software was used for data analysis, and p < 0.05 was considered as statistically significant difference. The differences between groups with normal and impaired single kidney function were compared using independent samples t-test. Furthermore, logistic regression was used to create and evaluate the predictive ability model of multiparametric MRI for single kidney function impairment. Stepwise method was used for Logistic regression. The area under the curve (AUC), sensitivity, and specificity were used to assess the diagnostic efficacy, and the cutoff value was derived. 3. Results A total of 62 patients with RAS were enrolled, and their baseline characteristics are presented in Table 1 . Six patients had unilateral renal atrophy, which affected the accuracy of MR parameter measurements. A total of 118 kidneys were finally included, the flowchart showed in Figure 1 . Representative multiparametric MR images are depicted in Figure 2 . Table 1. Baseline characteristics of the patients. Baseline characteristics Patients ( N = 62) Age (years) 63.3 ± 9.1 Sex (male/female) 34/28 BMI (kg/m 2 ) 25.0 ± 2.9 Systolic BP (mmHg) 141.3 ± 18.2 Diastolic BP (mmHg) 78.1 ± 11.7 Serum Creatinine (μmol/L) 97.5 ± 29.7 eGFR (mL/min/1.73 m 2 ) 68.4 ± 20.8 UA (μmol/L) 345.9 ± 76.6 ALB (g/L) 43.4 ± 4.0 TG (mmol/L) 1.5 ± 0.7 TC (mmol/L) 4.3 ± 1.4 HDL (mmol/L) 1.2 ± 0.3 LDL (mmol/L) 2.5 ± 1.1 Open in a new tab BMI: body mass index; BP: blood pressure; eGFR: estimated glomerular filtration rate; UA: uric acid; ALB: albumin; TG: triglyceride; TC: total cholesterol; HDL: high density lipoprotein; LDL: low density lipoprotein. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Flowchart of the study population enrollment. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Representative images of parametric maps obtained by multiparametric MRI showing (a) T1 mapping, (b) ASL perfusion, (c) T2* mapping, and (d) ADC. 3.1. Comparison of MR parameters between kidneys with normal and impaired single kidney renal function The 118 kidneys were categorized into normal (GFR ≥ 30) and reduced (GFR < 30) groups according to the isoSK-GFR [ 29 ], and the MR parameters were compared between the groups. The findings implied that renal PV, whole kidney RBF, cortical RBF, mean velocity, stroke volume, mean renal artery blood flow, cortical ADC, medullary ADC, and cortical and medullary T2* values in the reduced single kidney function group were significantly lower than those in the normal group, whereas the cortical T1 value was higher. On the contrary, medullary T1 value and corticomedullary difference (CMD) T1 did not differ significantly between the two groups. The observations are presented in Table 2 . Table 2. Differences in MR parameters between kidneys with normal and impaired single kidney renal function. MR parameters isoSK-GFR (mL/min) N Mean ± SD P -value Parenchymal Volume (cm 3 ) <30 40 97.8 ± 31.3 0.000 ≥30 78 144.9 ± 39.9 Whole Kidney RBF (mL/100 g/min) <30 40 144.9 ± 41.6 0.004 ≥30 78 174.0 ± 64.7 Cortical RBF (mL/100 g/min) <30 40 133.7 ± 45.2 0.000 ≥30 78 175.5 ± 76.6 Mean Velocity (cm/s) <30 40 8.2 ± 5.0 0.000 ≥30 78 14.1 ± 6.3 Stroke Volume (mL) <30 40 2.6 ± 1.6 0.000 ≥30 78 5.7 ± 2.5 Mean Flux (mL/s) <30 40 3.0 ± 1.7 0.000 ≥30 78 6.1 ± 2.4 Cortical ADC (10 −3 mm 2 /s) <30 40 1.8 ± 0.2 0.000 ≥30 78 2.0 ± 0.2 Medullary ADC (10 −3 mm 2 /s) <30 40 1.7 ± 0.2 0.000 ≥30 78 1.8 ± 0.2 Cortical T1 (ms) <30 40 1206.9 ± 111.1 0.000 ≥30 78 1117.3 ± 111.5 Medullary T1 (ms) <30 40 1489.3 ± 193.2 0.185 ≥30 78 1434.6 ± 219.5 CMD T1 (ms) <30 40 282.4 ± 123.1 0.194 ≥30 78 318.3 ± 149.3 Medullary T2* (ms) <30 40 29.7 ± 4.7 0.014 ≥30 78 31.8 ± 4.3 Cortical T2* (ms) <30 40 38.8 ± 6.4 0.000 ≥30 78 42.8 ± 5.0 Open in a new tab RBF: renal blood flow; ADC: apparent diffusion coefficient. 3.2. Logistic regression analysis of MR parameters to identify single kidney function impairment Univariate logistic regression analysis revealed that multiple MR parameters demonstrated good predictive ability for single kidney function impairment. Those with a strong predictive ability were predominantly hemodynamics and renal microstructure parameters. The strongest predictor was mean renal artery blood flow. The cutoff value was 3.24 mL/s, the sensitivity and specificity were 0.93 and 0.69, respectively, and the AUC was 0.864. The strongest predictors of impaired renal function among the microstructural parameters were cortical ADC and T1 value, with ROC AUCs of 0.756 and 0.741, cutoff values of 1.8285 × 10 −3 mm 2 /s and 1178 ms, sensitivities of 0.875 and 0.689, and specificities of 0.537 and 0.731, respectively. The results are listed in Table 3 and depicted in Figures 3a–c . Table 3. Univariate logistic analysis of the diagnostic efficacy of multiparametric MR for single kidney renal function impairment. MR parameters AUC Sensitivity Specificity Cutoff P -value Parenchyma Volume (cm 3 ) 0.821 0.86 0.67 104.30 0.000 Whole Kidney RBF (mL/100 g/min) 0.620 0.53 0.72 161.00 0.015 Cortical RBF (mL/100 g/min) 0.653 0.37 0.94 200.53 0.008 Mean Velocity (cm/s) 0.783 0.62 0.88 11.79 0.000 Stroke Volume (mL) 0.850 0.83 0.81 3.29 0.000 Mean Flux (mL/s) 0.864 0.93 0.69 3.24 0.000 Cortical ADC (10 −3 mm 2 /s) 0.756 0.88 0.54 1.83 0.000 Medullary ADC (10 −3 mm 2 /s) 0.693 0.44 0.87 1.83 0.001 Cortical T1 (ms) 0.741 0.69 0.73 1178.00 0.000 Cortical T2* (ms) 0.703 0.92 0.49 37.12 0.001 Open in a new tab RBF: renal blood flow; ADC: apparent diffusion coefficient. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Receiver operating characteristic curve analyses to identify single kidney function impairment using single MR parameters (a–c) and the model combining cortical RBF and cortical T1 (d). In the univariate logistic regression analysis, the AUC of each parameter for the diagnosis of impaired single kidney renal function was in the range of 0.597–0.864. A more optimized diagnostic model was obtained using multivariate logistic regression analysis with stepwise method by use all of parameters with a p value < 0.05 in Table 3 . In this model, multiparametric MRI that combined the renal cortical RBF and the cortical T1 value exhibited the highest diagnostic efficacy, with an AUC of 0.92, a sensitivity of 0.919, and a specificity of 0.743. The findings are illustrated in Figure 3d . This result was adjusted using the age parameter, and the model did not change, proving that the combined use of cortical RBF and cortical T1 did have a good diagnostic efficiency for unilateral renal dysfunction. 4. Discussion MRI is a noninvasive imaging technology with high soft-tissue resolution and does not involve ionizing radiation. In recent years, Non-Contrast MRI has been applied for the evaluation of kidney structure and function. Although innovative technologies such as PC-MRI and BOLD MRI have emerged, they are used independently to evaluate kidneys from a certain aspect and provide limited information. Recent results have shown that multiparametric MRI has potential in the evaluation of healthy kidneys, CKD, and renal allografts [ 30 ], which makes its application in other kidney diseases feasible. RAS affects renal hemodynamics, thus causing a series of serious secondary changes that require early intervention. However, previous studies have demonstrated that the renal function status and prognosis of kidneys with RAS are often not closely linked to RAS degree alone [ 31 , 32 ]. Previous investigations have attempted to provide more information to guide the treatment and prognosis of kidneys with RAS using MR and parameters derived from it. One example is the ratio of three-dimensional renal volume and SK-GFR. Such information is used to predict the prognosis of patients with RAS after renal artery stenting, and these studies have achieved some meaningful results [ 33 , 34 ]. Therefore, the evaluation of kidneys based on newer and more comprehensive MR technology is of immense significance for patients with RAS. GFR is routinely employed in clinical practice to understand the renal function of patients, especially isoSK-GFR, which can specifically reflect the renal function of a single kidney and has unique significance for patients with RAS [ 35 ]. However, as isoSK-GFR is an invasive examination with complex procedures, it imposes a heavy burden on patients and is not easy to achieve. The clinical diagnosis and treatment of patients with RAS have, in the past, mostly been focused on the extent of vascular stenosis and morphological characteristics, making it difficult to obtain renal function information. However, earlier investigations have shown that RAS does not represent impaired renal function [ 36 ]. A study implemented recently by our team discovered the correlation between multiple MRI parameters and GFR. The best prediction model for GFR using multiparametric MRI was obtained [ 37 ]. The predictive ability of multiparametric MRI for GFR enables the noninvasive evaluation of whether kidneys with RAS have renal function impairment. In this study, the 118 kidneys were categorized into two groups: normal and decreased renal function, and most parameters of multiparametric MRI differed significantly between the two groups. Univariate logistic regression analysis indicated that hemodynamic parameters had a strong predictive ability for RAS with single kidney function reduction, and the mean renal artery blood flow had the highest diagnostic efficiency (AUC = 0.864). The intricate relationship between renal artery hemodynamic parameters and renal function of kidneys with RAS is in line with pathophysiology. Moreover, Makvandi et al. [ 38 ] reported a strong correlation between mean renal artery blood flow and GFR in their study of diabetic kidney disease. Hence, it is difficult for the combination of other MR parameters to enhance the GFR predictive ability. Ischemic injury plays a key role in diverse types of kidney diseases, and thus, hemodynamic parameters are equally important in the assessment of renal function. In fact, PC-MRI has been shown many years ago to have high sensitivity and specificity for changes in renal artery hemodynamics. Schoenberg et al. performed PC-MRI in 23 patients with 48 RAS regions and compared MR Blood flow measurements with the severity of anatomic stenosis [ 8 ]. The results showed that PC-MRI had 100% sensitivity and 93% specificity in distinguishing patients with more than 50% renal artery stenosis from those without stenosis. PC-MRI is also based on the accurate response to hemodynamics, which makes it also has a good detection function for GFR. However, in our study, we did not treat it as a core parameter. Because it has an obvious limitation that PC-MRI parameters are not available for patients after renal artery stenting. In a series of studies, we would like to continue the follow-up evaluation of patients after renal-artery stenting, which is difficult with PC-MRI but not with other multiparametric MRIs of the renal parenchyma. According to our results, cortical T1 value also has considerable diagnostic efficiency for renal function impairment (AUC = 0.741). In the study by Hua et al. [ 39 ], combining T1 mapping and ADC value effectively predicted CKD and renal interstitial fibrosis (AUC = 0.96), which proves that multiparametric MRI can provide various diagnostic indicators and combination schemes from different aspects (hemodynamics, renal microstructure). When the kidney undergoes histological changes, such as cell swelling, cell membrane rupture, or enlarged interstitial space, water molecular diffusion can be limited or enhanced, resulting in changes in ADC values. The decrease of ADC values is also associated with the progression of renal fibrosis, where the deposition of fibroblasts and collagen in the interstitium reduces the diffusion movement of water molecules [ 40 ]. Although the cortical T2* value shows diagnostic efficacy for unilateral renal dysfunction in univariate Logistic regression (AUC = 0.703), its specificity is low (specificity =0.49). This outcome is influenced by numerous factors that impact the level of renal oxygenation. The decrease in T2* value does not necessarily represent a decrease in renal function, but may also be due to increased oxygen consumption due to increased tubular reabsorption. Besides, because of the differences in oxygen pressure states of the renal cortex and medulla, they are located in different positions of the oxygen dissociation curve, which also makes the alterations in renal T2* value more complex than other parameters [ 41 ]. In our study, multivariate logistic regression analysis showed that the combined use of multiparametric MRI indicators could enhance the diagnostic efficacy of renal function impairment, and the combination of cortical RBF and cortical T1 values exhibited the highest efficacy (AUC = 0.92). A previous study of CKD by Buchanan et al. [ 42 ] found that the use of renal cortical perfusion combined with cortical T1 value in multiparametric MRI was the strongest predicter of GFR, which agrees with our findings. T1 value can reflect tissue water content and fibrosis degree. When RAS occurs, due to the reduction of renal blood flow, renal tissue ischemia and hypoxia may lead to fibrosis and other pathological changes. Collagen deposition in the intercellular space leads to the limitation of interstitial water molecules movement and the increase of T1 value [ 43 ]. Our study established that multiparametric MRI can not only predict GFR in patients with CKD but also has a considerable diagnostic efficiency for RAS renal function impairment. In our study, information on kidney macroscopic and microscopic structure and function was collected using a noninvasive multiparametric MRI sequence that takes only 20 min for a single acquisition. According to the characteristics of patients with RAS, the prediction ability of single kidney function impairment was evaluated. Moreover, the sample size was expanded compared with the previous multiparametric MRI study. Nevertheless, our study has certain limitations. First of all, owing to the close association between RAS and hypertension, some patients may be complicated with hypertension, and its influencing factors cannot be completely excluded while enrolling patients with RAS. Second, patients with CKD, diabetes, and AKI were excluded via medical history and clinical biochemical examination. However, owing to the occult nature of some renal lesions in the early stage, it cannot be guaranteed that the kidneys of patients with RAS were completely free of renal parenchymal changes caused by factors other than RAS. In isoSK-GFR reduced (GFR < 30) group, no obvious stenosis was found in the corresponding renal artery in 5 kidneys. We believe that these kidneys should be affected by CKD. Third, because of the process characteristics of clinical diagnosis and treatment of patients with RAS, the histological gold standard to verify the MRI results was lacking. Fourth, though respiratory triggering was added in DWI sequence and breath-hold was implement in BOLD sequence, there is still possibilities for residual motions. This may have some impact on the accuracy of post-processing. Finally, although the sample size was large compared with previous studies, owing to the large number of indicators used in multiparametric MRI, to further enhance the accuracy of the prediction model, the sample size should be expanded in subsequent studies. In conclusion, this study shows multiparametric MRI potential to detect single renal function reduction in kidneys with RAS, which holds promise for the diagnosis and prognosis of treatment outcomes in patients with RAS. Supplementary Material Supplementary Material.docx IRNF_A_2444403_SM1991.docx (152.3KB, docx) figure 2.tif IRNF_A_2444403_SM1990.tif (5.1MB, tif) figure 1.tif IRNF_A_2444403_SM1989.tif (1.2MB, tif) figure 3.tif IRNF_A_2444403_SM1988.tif (1.6MB, tif) Funding Statement This study was supported by National Key Technologies Research and Development Program of China (2022YFE0209800). Disclosure statement All authors have no conflicts of interest to disclose. References 1. Haller ST, Evans KL, Folt DA, et al. Mechanisms and treatments for renal artery stenosis. Discov Med. 2013;16(90):255–260. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Renal artery stenosis: epidemiology and treatment. Int J Nephrol Renovasc Dis. 2014;7:169–181. doi: 10.2147/ijnrd.S40175. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Van Twist DJL. Improving intrarenal microcirculation prior to balloon angioplasty: new chances for the treatment of atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis? J Hypertens. 2019;37(10):1963–1965. doi: 10.1097/HJH.0000000000002167. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Sures